{"content_hash": "8cb68de5e5c6fd3e", "version_id": 346, "article_id": 35, "level_code": "ja_n5", "lang": "ja", "level": 1, "level_label": "N5 初級", "title": "みえない ぼうりょく：こころの びょうきの ひとが おこす さつじんの ひがいしゃは かぞく", "title_zh": "看不见的暴力：精神疾病患者杀人，受害者多是自家人", "has_translation": true, "topic": "health", "source": "medical_x", "source_url": "https://medicalxpress.com/news/2026-10-hidden-violence-family-members-main.html", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1495873872334-b0882b96cd95?ixid=M3wxMDk3Mzk1fDB8MXxzZWFyY2h8OHx8aGlkZGVuJTIwdmlvbGVuY2V8ZW58MHwwfHx8MTc5MTU1MDMwMXww&ixlib=rb-4.1.0&w=1600&q=75&fm=jpg&fit=max", "provider": "unsplash", "credit": "Photo by Ben Maguire on Unsplash", "author": "Ben Maguire", "credit_url": "https://unsplash.com/@bmaguire530?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "source": "Unsplash", "source_url": "https://unsplash.com/photos/person-covering-face-with-right-hand-3AE3jUCBhxo?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "fallback": {"type": "gradient", "from": "hsl(93, 42%, 62%)", "to": "hsl(131, 46%, 48%)", "label": "HEALTH", "seed": "51edab589bfb"}}, "published_date": "2026-10-09", "word_count": 363, "reading_minutes": 1.1, "lead": "スペインの 大学が さつじんの データを しらべました。こころの びょうきの ひとが おこす さつじんは、ほとんど いえの なかで おきます。ひがいしゃの 多くは かぞくです。", "lead_zh": "西班牙一所大学调查了杀人案件的数据。精神疾病患者犯下的杀人案，大多发生在家里。受害者很多是家人。", "preview": "スペインの 大学の 人たちが、10年の あいだの さつじんの じけんを しらべました。あつめた じけんは 2500より 多いです。その 10パーセントで、おこした 人に こころの びょうきが ありました。", "preview_zh": "西班牙一所大学的研究人员调查了十年间的杀人案件。收集的案件超过2500件。其中10%的案件里，作案人有精神疾病。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 336, "url": "https://pub-aba43a6fb1db4dc08fede1dbc81f3241.r2.dev/audio/35/ja_n5.mp3", "duration": 68.212, "size_bytes": 412992, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "ja-JP-NanamiNeural", "word_count": 212, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@ba94379/content/data/versions/346.json", "paragraphs": ["スペインの 大学の 人たちが、10年の あいだの さつじんの じけんを しらべました。あつめた じけんは 2500より 多いです。その 10パーセントで、おこした 人に こころの びょうきが ありました。", "びょうきの ある 人が おこす さつじんは、ほとんど いえの なかで おきます。26パーセントは、おやを ころす じけんです。かぞくは いちばん そばに いる 人です。だから、いちばん あぶないです。", "でも、ひがいしゃに おかねは あまり 出ません。かぞくの なかの ことなので、国は あまり たすけません。ひがいしゃは じぶんで べんごしを やとって、かぞくを うったえる ことも あります。", "けんきゅうの 人は 言います。「こころの びょうきの 人を わるく 言わない ように する ことは 大切です。でも、その ために ひがいしゃの ことが 見えなく なって います」と 言います。", "これからは、びょういんが かぞくを もっと 見る ことが 大切です。あぶない 人を 早く 見つけて、かぞくを たすける しくみが ひつようです。"], "paragraphs_zh": ["西班牙一所大学的研究人员调查了十年间的杀人案件。收集的案件超过2500件。其中10%的案件里，作案人有精神疾病。", "有精神疾病的人犯下的杀人案，大多发生在家里。26%是杀害父母的案件。家人是离得最近的人，所以最危险。", "但是，受害者很少得到赔偿。因为是家里的事，国家帮助得不多。受害者有时要自己请律师，起诉家人。", "研究人员说：“不把精神疾病患者说成坏人，这很重要。但正因为这样，受害者的处境被忽视了。”", "今后，医院要更多地关注家属。需要建立机制，尽早发现有风险的人，帮助家人。"], "body": "スペインの 大学の 人たちが、10年の あいだの さつじんの じけんを しらべました。あつめた じけんは 2500より 多いです。その 10パーセントで、おこした 人に こころの びょうきが ありました。\n\nびょうきの ある 人が おこす さつじんは、ほとんど いえの なかで おきます。26パーセントは、おやを ころす じけんです。かぞくは いちばん そばに いる 人です。だから、いちばん あぶないです。\n\nでも、ひがいしゃに おかねは あまり 出ません。かぞくの なかの ことなので、国は あまり たすけません。ひがいしゃは じぶんで べんごしを やとって、かぞくを うったえる ことも あります。\n\nけんきゅうの 人は 言います。「こころの びょうきの 人を わるく 言わない ように する ことは 大切です。でも、その ために ひがいしゃの ことが 見えなく なって います」と 言います。\n\nこれからは、びょういんが かぞくを もっと 見る ことが 大切です。あぶない 人を 早く 見つけて、かぞくを たすける しくみが ひつようです。", "body_zh": "西班牙一所大学的研究人员调查了十年间的杀人案件。收集的案件超过2500件。其中10%的案件里，作案人有精神疾病。\n\n有精神疾病的人犯下的杀人案，大多发生在家里。26%是杀害父母的案件。家人是离得最近的人，所以最危险。\n\n但是，受害者很少得到赔偿。因为是家里的事，国家帮助得不多。受害者有时要自己请律师，起诉家人。\n\n研究人员说：“不把精神疾病患者说成坏人，这很重要。但正因为这样，受害者的处境被忽视了。”\n\n今后，医院要更多地关注家属。需要建立机制，尽早发现有风险的人，帮助家人。", "text": "スペインの 大学の 人たちが、10年の あいだの さつじんの じけんを しらべました。あつめた じけんは 2500より 多いです。その 10パーセントで、おこした 人に こころの びょうきが ありました。 びょうきの ある 人が おこす さつじんは、ほとんど いえの なかで おきます。26パーセントは、おやを ころす じけんです。かぞくは いちばん そばに いる 人です。だから、いちばん あぶないです。 でも、ひがいしゃに おかねは あまり 出ません。かぞくの なかの ことなので、国は あまり たすけません。ひがいしゃは じぶんで べんごしを やとって、かぞくを うったえる ことも あります。 けんきゅうの 人は 言います。「こころの びょうきの 人を わるく 言わない ように する ことは 大切です。でも、その ために ひがいしゃの ことが 見えなく なって います」と 言います。 これからは、びょういんが かぞくを もっと 見る ことが 大切です。あぶない 人を 早く 見つけて、かぞくを たすける しくみが ひつようです。", "vocab": [{"word": "さつじん", "pos": "noun", "zh": "杀人", "note": "「さつじんの じけん」＝杀人案件"}, {"word": "こころの びょうき", "pos": "phrase", "zh": "精神疾病", "note": "心の病気，心理或精神方面的病"}, {"word": "ひがいしゃ", "pos": "noun", "zh": "受害者", "note": "被害者，受到伤害的人"}, {"word": "かぞく", "pos": "noun", "zh": "家人，家属", "note": "家族，家庭成员"}, {"word": "いえ", "pos": "noun", "zh": "家，房子", "note": "「いえの なか」＝家里"}, {"word": "おかね", "pos": "noun", "zh": "钱", "note": "お金，这里指赔偿金"}, {"word": "べんごし", "pos": "noun", "zh": "律师", "note": "弁護士，帮人打官司的人"}, {"word": "たすける", "pos": "verb", "zh": "帮助，救助", "note": "助ける，帮助有困难的人"}], "title_original": "Hidden violence: Family members as the main victims of homicides linked to mental disorders", "summary_original": "In recent decades, the social and legal perception of people with mental disorders who commit crimes has changed. Initially, people with mental disorders were considered dangerous. However, since the late 20th century, the paradigm has shifted at both social and legal levels, with people experiencing mental disorders often considered victims themselves, despite having committed intentional crimes.", "body_original": "Hidden violence: Family members as the main victims of homicides linked to mental disorders\nby\nOpen University of Catalonia\nedited by\nLisa Lock\n, reviewed by\nRobert Egan\nEditors' notes\nThis article has been reviewed according to Science X's\neditorial process\nand\npolicies\n.\nEditors\nhave highlighted\nthe following attributes while ensuring the content's credibility:\nfact-checked\ntrusted source\nproofread\nThe GIST\nAdd to Preferred Sources\nIn recent decades, the social and legal perception of people with mental disorders who commit crimes has changed. Initially, people with mental disorders were considered dangerous. However, since the late 20th century, the paradigm has shifted at both social and legal levels, with people experiencing mental disorders often considered victims themselves, despite having committed intentional crimes.\nIn this new context, a team of researchers from the Universitat Oberta de Catalunya (UOC) has analyzed homicides committed in Spain to characterize those perpetrated by people with mental disorders and study the factors that determine who the victims are and the compensation awarded in legal proceedings. Their research is\npublished\nin the\nJournal of Forensic and Legal Medicine\n.\n\"The noble aim of avoiding stigmatizing people with mental disorders as dangerous can contribute to overlooking the serious situation of those who are victims of this violence or face a greater risk of victimization,\" explained Josep Maria Tamarit, a professor of criminal law at the UOC and coordinator of the Empirical and Applied Victimology research group (VICRIM). He coordinated this research together with professors Laura Arantegui and Alba Marsol, who are part of the same group. All three teach in the Faculty of Law and Political Science.\nAnalysis of 2,500 homicides in a decade\nAs part of the study, the researchers from the Digital Transformation and Governance Research Center (UOC-DIGIT) reviewed more than 2,500 homicide cases tried in Spanish courts. Of these cases, 10% involved perpetrators with some type of mental disorder. In the vast majority of cases, homicides committed by people with some type of mental disorder occurred in the home, with 26% being parricides (involving the killing of a parent).\nAccording to the experts, the main objective of this open-access study was to shed light on a reality that has so far been overlooked: the lack of recognition and compensation for victims of lethal violence committed by people with mental disorders, an issue obscured by efforts to prevent the stigmatization of the perpetrators. In addition, the study found that only a small number of victims of such crimes received compensation or financial support, a disadvantage largely attributable to the intrafamilial nature of most incidents.\nThe issue is complex: When a person with a mental disorder intentionally assaults a family member, the consequences include both physical and psychological harm. Other family members are also affected.\nFamily ties as the greatest risk factor\nHowever, in many cases, because of this tendency to avoid stigmatizing people with mental disorders, perpetrators are acquitted or receive minimal sentences. Meanwhile, victims, both direct and indirect, are left without any compensation.\n\"Family members are most at risk because they are the people who have the greatest contact with perpetrators. Society leaves this problem in the hands of families. It is also important to consider the tensions that can arise within family relationships, which are intensified by the stress caused by living with people with these types of disorders,\" explained Tamarit, who noted that these victims \"do not receive sufficient support from institutions.\"\nThis legal and contextual situation can be \"considered a systemic failure\" because victims are not compensated. Moreover, in some cases, to obtain effective compensation, they must hire a lawyer and pursue criminal proceedings against a family member with a mental disorder.\n\"We don't have any data to report specific failures, but if one considers that more than 10% of homicides are committed by people who represent no more than 2% of the population, and whose mental health problems are known to the health care system in most cases, we should be able to improve the detection of cases involving risk and the monitoring of individuals and their families,\" said the UOC researcher.\nPreventive and reparative measures\nTo prevent this type of homicide, particularly within the family setting, support for families provided by the public health care system should be strengthened, including follow-up care for cases where risk has been identified.\nIn the experts' view, it is essential to rethink the current approach to victimization and to focus on the specific characteristics of this form of victimization, which frequently occurs within the perpetrator's closest family circle.\n\"The health care system should increase the monitoring of cases, apply risk identification tools and improve the public support system for victims of violent crime. While several reforms have been introduced to improve support in cases of gender-based violence, other needs have so far remained overlooked,\" concluded Tamarit.\nMore information\nJosep Maria Tamarit et al, Invisible harm after lethal violence: Victims of homicide by offenders with mental disorders,\nJournal of Forensic and Legal Medicine\n(2026).\nDOI: 10.1016/j.jflm.2026.103173\nKey medical concepts\nMental Disorders\nHomicide\nClinical categories\nPsychiatry\nPsychology & Mental health\nProvided by\nOpen University of Catalonia\nWho's behind this story?\nLisa Lock\nBA art history, MA material culture. Former museum editor, paramedic, and transplant coordinator. Editing for Science X since 2021.\nFull profile →\nRobert Egan\nBachelor's in mathematical biology, Master's in creative writing. Well-traveled with unique perspectives on science and language.\nFull profile →\nCitation\n:\nHidden violence: Family members as the main victims of homicides linked to mental disorders (2026, October 8)\nretrieved 8 October 2026\nfrom https://medicalxpress.com/news/2026-10-hidden-violence-family-members-main.html\nThis document is subject to copyright. Apart from any fair dealing for the purpose of private study or research, no\npart may be reproduced without the written permission. The content is provided for information purposes only.", "fetched_at": "2026-10-09T01:22:31.644417", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "スペイン", "s": 0.125, "e": 0.613, "sm": 125, "em": 612, "cs": 0, "ce": 4, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "の", "s": 0.625, "e": 0.75, "sm": 625, "em": 750, "cs": 4, "ce": 5, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "大学", "s": 0.762, "e": 1.225, "sm": 762, "em": 1225, "cs": 6, "ce": 8, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "の", "s": 1.238, "e": 1.325, "sm": 1237, "em": 1325, "cs": 8, "ce": 9, "si": 0}, {"i": 4, "w": "人", "s": 1.33